</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/17:_Alcohols_and_Phenols/17.07:_Oxidation_of_Alcohols" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Mechanism"></span><h3 class="editable">Mechanism</h3>

<p>During this reaction mechanism the chromium atom is being reduced from Cr(VI) in the CrO<sub>3</sub> starting material to Cr(IV) in the H<sub>2</sub>CrO<sub>3</sub> product. Also, notice the the C=O bond is formed in the third step of the mechanism through an E2 reaction. Although E2 reaction are generally know for forming C=C double bonds thought the elimination of a halide leaving group, in this case they are use to generate a C=O through the elimination of a reduced metal as a leaving group.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36351"><img class="internal" alt="17.7 jones oxid.png" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/273594/17.7_jones_oxid.png?revision=1" /></p>


